Chapter: NetDriver
  cites Kernel chapter Ne2k

 The one place the I/O loop names a NIC.

 NetIO used to call ne2k-send-frame and ne2k-recv-frame directly, at six
 sites across four loops. That made the NE2000 not the default network
 card but the only one the stack could express: adding a second card
 meant editing every loop that moves a frame, and every one of those
 loops is fuel-capped, self-tail-recursive and bracketed by
 __heap-save/__heap-restore, so they are the last functions in the stack
 anyone should be editing for a reason unrelated to what they do.

 So the name moves here. NetIO calls net-driver-send-frame and
 net-driver-recv-frame; those call the NE2000. The loops no longer
 mention a card.

 THERE IS ONE DRIVER BEHIND THIS SEAM AND IT IS THE NE2000. This
 chapter is not a registry, it does not probe, and it does not choose --
 saying otherwise would be a capability this project has not built.
 What it is, is the single edit point: a second card becomes a branch in
 the two functions below, and nothing above them changes.

 What a second card costs, stated so nobody rediscovers it: the NE2000
 is not really driven from Codex at all. ne2k-send-frame and
 ne2k-recv-frame delegate to the net-send-raw / net-recv-raw builtins,
 whose register programming is hand-emitted machine code in the
 compiler (Emit/X86_64IPCHelpers.codex) against a fixed port base. A
 driver for real hardware does not follow that route and should not:
 VirtioNet is the shape that works from here, a chapter of ordinary
 Codex over peek-32 and poke-32 against a BAR, needing no builtin and
 no compiler change. That is why this seam sits above the builtins
 rather than beside them.

Section: Send

  net-driver-send-frame : List Integer -> [Network.Write] Integer
  net-driver-send-frame (frame) = ne2k-send-frame frame

Section: Receive

 The receive path allocates a list on every miss and is polled up to
 fifty million times per accept, so this delegation must stay a plain
 call that returns what it is given. It adds a frame to the stack and
 allocates nothing of its own; the __heap-save/__heap-restore pair that
 makes those polls survivable stays in NetIO, wrapped around this call
 exactly as it was wrapped around the driver's.

  net-driver-recv-frame : [Network.Read] List Integer
  net-driver-recv-frame = ne2k-recv-frame

